## Introduction

This article describes the implementation of a dual-instance host USB application on an STM32 microcontroller. The goal is to demonstrate that each USB instance operates independently and manages its own class.

This article provides a step-by-step workflow to set up, generate, and adapt the USB application. [STM32CubeMX](https://www.st.com/en/development-tools/stm32cubemx.html) is used to generate the code with CMake and [STM32CubeIDE](https://www.st.com/en/development-tools/stm32cubeide.html) is used to develop the application. It covers USB host core, class assignment to each instance, project generation, and integration in a STM32CubeIDE environment.

For this article, the [STM32H7S78-DK](https://www.st.com/en/evaluation-tools/stm32h7s78-dk.html) board is used to implement this application. This board features an [STM32H7S7L8](https://www.st.com/en/microcontrollers-microprocessors/stm32h7s7l8.html) microcontroller, which integrates a dual-instance USB core (USB High-Speed and USB Full-Speed). For further details about the board, refer to the [*user manual.*](https://www.st.com/resource/en/user_manual/um3289-discovery-kit-with-stm32h7s7l8-mcu-stmicroelectronics.pdf)

